==English==

===Etymology===

In England, during the plague, coffins were often unburied and reused. After discovering that a great deal of the coffins had scratch marks on the ''inside'''', the dead were buried with a string tied to their fingers and attached to a bell above ground. That way, if a person was buried alive, the ringing bell would alert the mourners. In common use, it is inferred that the person in question could have in fact been a "dead ringer", explaining why they look so much like the one they are being compared to.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|sg=[[dead]] [[ringer]]}}

# {{idiom}} Someone or something that very closely resembles another; someone or something easily mistaken for another.
#:''He is a '''dead ringer''' for his grandfather at that age.''

